\details{
\strong{Same output format.} The result is the same kind of named integer
vector as the static attack (names = vertex or edge names, values =
indices in the \emph{original} graph), so it can be passed unchanged to
\code{\link[=robustness]{robustness()}}, \code{\link[=edge_robustness]{edge_robustness()}} or used inside the \code{attack_fn} of
\code{\link[=robustness_null_structural]{robustness_null_structural()}}, e.g.
\code{attack_fn = function(g) attack_by_degree(g, recalculate = TRUE)}.

\strong{Same options at every step.} Each step calls the static attack on the
current graph with the same arguments (\code{directed}, \code{mode}, \code{weights},
\code{weights_as}, \code{which}, ...). An explicit \code{weights} vector is matched to
its edges, so it shrinks with the network as edges disappear. Ties are
broken exactly as in the static attack (the lower original index first;
see \code{\link[=attack_by_hits]{attack_by_hits()}} for its own tie-break).

\strong{HITS and reproducibility.} As the network shrinks, a recalculated HITS
attack often reaches small subgraphs (e.g. a chain A -> B -> C) whose
leading eigenvalue is repeated. There the scores are not unique, and
ARPACK's random starting vector decides which solution is returned, so
the removal order can change between runs even though ties are broken
deterministically. Call \code{set.seed()} before the attack for a
reproducible order. Degree, betweenness, closeness, eigenvector and
PageRank attacks do not depend on the random seed.

\strong{No edges left.} Once the remaining vertices have no edges, every
centrality is zero or undefined, so the rest are appended in original
index order without further computation.

\strong{Warnings} raised while recomputing (e.g. the eigenvector-to-PageRank
fallback, or an argument that has no effect) are collected and issued
once each, not once per step.

\strong{Cost.} A recalculated attack computes the centrality up to \emph{n} times
instead of once. For degree this is cheap; for betweenness and closeness
(each roughly O(n m) per computation on a graph with \emph{n} vertices and \emph{m}
edges) the whole attack is roughly O(n^2 m), and inside
\code{\link[=robustness_null_structural]{robustness_null_structural()}} it is repeated for every replicate. Time a
single call on your network before running large null models.

\code{attack_by_edge_weight()} has no \code{recalculate} option: an edge's weight
does not change when other edges are removed, so recalculating would
